// From the designer: pink 40% of the height, purple 20% and blue 40%, so 2:1:2. On 3:5.
vexyl 1
meta {
  name "Bisexual Pride Flag"
  designer "Michael Page"
  encoder "Naomi Persephone Amethyst"
  license "CC-BY-SA-4.0"
  date "1998-12-05"
  source "https://web.archive.org/web/20011204204150/http://biflag.com:80/Activism.asp?tag=EN000000000000"
  source "https://web.archive.org/web/20090522164814/http://www.biflag.com/5img/logos/BiPrideFlagTrueColorSize.gif"
  source "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bisexual_pride_flag"
  source "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bisexual_Pride_Flag.svg"
  note "Designer's own page, The History of the Bi Pride Flag by Michael Page on BiFlag.com, read in the Wayback Machine's copy of 4 December 2001. It says the first Bi Pride Flag was unveiled on 5 December 1998, and that the flag is for free public usage."
  note "From the page: the top 40% of the vertical dimension is pink, the middle 20% purple and the lower 40% blue, in flag-maker parlance magenta PMS 226, lavender PMS 258 and royal PMS 286. It asks for those exact PMS colors."
  note "The hex values are sampled from BiPrideFlagTrueColorSize.gif on the designer's site, archived in 2009 with no page around it: #d60270, #9b4f96 and #0038a8, in stripes of 60, 30 and 60 pixels. The Commons drawing has the same values."
  note "From elsewhere: the proportion. The page gives none. That image is 200 by 150, 3:4, which isn't stated as the flag's shape. 3:5 is the common format, as in Commons."
}
palette {
  magenta "#d60270" {
    pantone "226"
  }
  lavender "#9b4f96" {
    pantone "258"
  }
  royal "#0038a8" {
    pantone "286"
  }
}
proportion hoist=3 fly=5

bars magenta lavender royal {
  ratio 2 1 2
}
